## Ground rules

- **Conventions are lint-enforced.** `dude lint` runs rules AF001–AF010;
  each has a page in `.claude/rules/AF/NNN.md`. Read the relevant page before
  fixing a violation.
- **One DAG per file**, file named after the `dag_id`, under `airflow/dags/`
  (AF001). Shared helpers go in `airflow/dags/lib/` — never a second DAG in
  an existing file.
- Every DAG: explicit `schedule=` (AF002), explicit `catchup=` (AF003),
  non-empty `tags=` (AF004), `default_args=DEFAULT_ARGS` from `lib.defaults`
  (AF005).
- **Parse-time hygiene**: no `Variable.get`/connection lookups outside task
  functions (AF006); heavy imports (pandas, numpy, …) inside task functions
  (AF007).
- Plugins are packages under `airflow/plugins/<name>/` registering an
  `AirflowPlugin` subclass in `__init__.py` (AF008). `ops_toolkit/` is the
  reference.
- New Python deps: **pinned** lines in `airflow/requirements.txt` (AF009),
  then `dude up --build`.
- Every env var consumed anywhere must be documented in `.env.example`
  (AF010). Real secrets: `.env` locally{{#if withIac}}, `dude iac secrets` in AWS{{/if}}.

## Validation loop

```bash
dude lint          # structural conventions (fast, no Airflow needed)
dude test          # DAG integrity suite (runs pytest inside the image)
dude dag errors    # live import errors from the running deployment
dude format        # ruff format + import sort
```

Run `dude lint && dude test` before considering any change done.

## Architecture notes

- All components (api-server :8080, scheduler, dag-processor, triggerer) run
  the SAME image built from `airflow/` — compose bind-mounts `dags/` and
  `plugins/`, so DAG edits are live; plugin and dependency changes need a
  restart / rebuild respectively.
- Sign-on is configured in `airflow/config/webserver_config.py`
  ({{#if withEntraId}}Entra ID OAuth — docs/docs/sso.md{{else}}native DB auth{{/if}}).
- The example DAGs (`airflow/dags/examples/`) demonstrate: TaskFlow ETL,
  dedicated-container execution on ECS and Kubernetes, and a simulated AWS
  Batch compute-intensive pattern. They self-guard so they succeed (skip or
  simulate) without cloud infrastructure — preserve that property when
  editing them.
{{#if withIac}}
- `iac/terraform` deploys to AWS ECS Fargate with **hybrid executors**:
  ordinary tasks run via LocalExecutor inside the scheduler container (no
  Fargate cold start); a task opts into its own dedicated Fargate container
  with `executor=os.getenv("DEDICATED_TASK_EXECUTOR")` (ECS executor,
  60–90s cold start — reserve for heavy work). Task logs go to S3. DAGs are
  baked into the image at `dude iac ship` — there is no live mount in AWS.
  Monitoring: `dude iac status`, `dude iac logs --service <component>`, and
  the CloudWatch `dashboard_url` output. See docs/docs/deploy.md.
{{/if}}
